sections.s2_technical:
`@MappedSuperclass` is purely a code-reuse mechanism for mappings. You put common fields — an id strategy, audit columns like createdAt/updatedAt, a version column — on a base class annotated `@MappedSuperclass`, and each `@Entity` subclass inherits those fields as columns in its *own* table. The base class is not an entity: there is no table for it, you cannot write `em.find(BaseClass.class, id)`, you cannot run `SELECT b FROM BaseClass b`, and no relationship can target it. Each subclass is completely independent at the database level; they just happen to share column definitions.

```java
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @Column(name = "created_at") private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    @Column(name = "created_by") private String createdBy;
}

@Entity
public class Account extends Auditable { @Id private String id; /* gets created_at, created_by */ }

@Entity
public class LoanApplication extends Auditable { @Id private String id; /* also gets them */ }
```

`@Inheritance` creates a genuine polymorphic hierarchy. The base class is `@Entity` and carries `@Inheritance(strategy = ...)`; subclasses are entities. Now the base type is queryable: `SELECT a FROM Account a` returns Savings and Current accounts together, and an association `@ManyToOne Account account` can point to either subtype. This is what you need when the subtypes are real variants you treat uniformly in some contexts and specifically in others.

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
public abstract class Account { @Id protected String id; protected BigDecimal balance; }

@Entity public class SavingsAccount extends Account { private BigDecimal interestRate; }
@Entity public class CurrentAccount extends Account { private BigDecimal overdraftLimit; }
```

The decision: if you only want to avoid repeating audit columns and never need to treat the base as a type, use `@MappedSuperclass` — it is simpler and each table is clean. If you need polymorphic queries ("all accounts regardless of subtype"), polymorphic associations, or runtime type dispatch, use `@Inheritance`.

Senior nuances: `@MappedSuperclass` can define mapped relationships and `@PrePersist`/`@PreUpdate` callbacks that subclasses inherit, which is a common place to stamp audit fields. With `@MappedSuperclass` you can override inherited column mappings in a subclass via `@AttributeOverride`. Choosing `@Inheritance` then forces a second decision — the strategy (SINGLE_TABLE, JOINED, TABLE_PER_CLASS) — each with different table shapes and performance. And you can combine them: a `@MappedSuperclass` for audit fields plus an `@Inheritance` hierarchy for the real types.

